```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;

public class UserLookup {

    // FIXED: Uses a PreparedStatement with parameterized placeholders,
    // which ensures user input is treated as data, not executable SQL.
    public static String findUser(Connection conn, String username) throws Exception {
        // GOOD: The query structure is fixed; '?' is a bind parameter
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
        PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(query);

        // Set the parameter safely — the JDBC driver handles escaping
        pstmt.setString(1, username);

        ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();

        if (rs.next()) {
            return rs.getString("email");
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

**Why the fix works:**

| Aspect | Bad (vulnerable) | Good (fixed) |
|---|---|---|
| Query construction | String concatenation | Parameterized `?` placeholder |
| Input handling | Raw user string injected into SQL | `setString()` lets the driver escape/quote the value |
| Attack example | `username = "'; DROP TABLE users; --"` | The same input is sent as a literal string value, never parsed as SQL |

The key principle: **never build SQL (or any interpreter language) by concatenating untrusted input**. Always use the API's parameterized / prepared-statement mechanism so the database engine can distinguish between code and data.

